---
# registry has no REGISTRY_AUTH (see deployment.yaml.j2) and its Service has no auth in front of
# it either — the loopback hostPort only protects the LAN path. Any pod in {{ k8s_namespace }}
# can currently push or delete images over the ClusterIP, including qbittorrent's and jellyfin's
# untrusted-input neighbors. This narrows that to the pods that legitimately write here.
#
# INGRESS, deliberately — the direction proven enforced on this cluster. Egress policies select
# pods correctly and block nothing (measured 2026-08-07, sonarr's isolation-probe header); this
# repeats the same choice prowlarr's flaresolverr policy and n8n's broker policy already made,
# and netpol-probe-job.yaml.j2 beside this file checks it on every deploy rather than assuming.
#
# TWO ADMIT RULES, for two different kinds of traffic:
#
#   1. POD-NETWORK PUSHERS — image-builder's build Jobs (any image_builder_name, admitted via
#      the shared `netpol-role: image-builder` label added in
#      image-builder/templates/build-job.yaml.j2 for exactly this) and this role's own
#      selftest-push/selftest-pull Jobs. Ordinary podSelector matching; unremarkable.
#
#   2. THE PULL PATH — containerd resolves {{ k8s_registry_pull_host }} (localhost, the loopback
#      hostPort in deployment.yaml.j2) and is a HOST process, not a pod. That traffic never
#      carries a pod identity a podSelector could match, so it needs an ipBlock. See the ipBlock
#      comment below for what is and is not proven about it.
#
# WHAT DOES NOT NEED A RULE: the readinessProbe/livenessProbe on this same port. n8n's broker
# policy left its probed port (5678) open to ALL sources specifically because kube-router's
# handling of node-sourced probe traffic under a restrictive policy was untested at the time it
# was written. It is no longer untested: flaresolverr's policy restricts its ONLY port (8191) to
# `podSelector: {app: prowlarr}` with no exception of any kind, and that pod has run 1/1 Ready
# for 4 days on readiness+liveness probes over that exact port (checked 2026-08-13). Kubelet
# probes a pod's IP directly — not through the hostPort/loopback path — and kube-router does not
# subject that path to ingress enforcement. This policy relies on the same proven behavior rather
# than re-deriving it, and does not carve out {{ k8s_registry_port }} for probes.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: registry
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: registry
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    # Pod-network pushers: image-builder's build Jobs and this role's own selftest Jobs.
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              netpol-role: image-builder
        - podSelector:
            matchExpressions:
              - key: app
                operator: In
                values:
                  - registry-selftest-push
                  - registry-selftest-pull
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: {{ k8s_registry_port }}
    # The PULL path. containerd on daniel-box is a HOST process reaching the pod through the
    # loopback hostPort (portmap), not a pod on the cluster network — a podSelector cannot admit
    # it by identity, only an ipBlock by address can.
    #
    # PROVEN: `ip -4 addr show cni0` on daniel-box reads 10.42.0.1/24, matching the node's live
    # podCIDR (`kubectl get node daniel-box -o jsonpath='{.spec.podCIDR}'` = 10.42.0.0/24). That
    # is the standard hostPort/portmap SNAT target — the address hostPort-forwarded traffic is
    # rewritten to before it reaches the pod netns, so return traffic routes back through cni0.
    #
    # NOT PROVEN, and stated plainly rather than papered over: this repo has no prior
    # hostPort+NetworkPolicy combination to test against (every other fenced pod in this cluster
    # is reached over its Service or a normal pod IP, never a hostPort), and applying this policy
    # for a live test is out of scope for this change (no cluster writes). Three outcomes are
    # possible after a real deploy, not two:
    #   (a) this ipBlock is exactly right — the pull succeeds because the source matches;
    #   (b) the SNAT target is some other address — the pull fails, this ipBlock needs widening;
    #   (c) hostPort-DNAT'd traffic bypasses kube-router's ingress enforcement entirely, the same
    #       way kubelet's direct-to-pod-IP probe traffic does (see the probe paragraph above) —
    #       the pull succeeds, but this ipBlock is inert: neither protecting nor admitting
    #       anything, and should be deleted so it doesn't read as load-bearing to the next
    #       person who edits this policy.
    # registry-selftest-pull-job.yaml.j2 already runs on every deploy and distinguishes (a)/(c)
    # from (b) for free: (b) fails loudly (`sudo k3s kubectl -n {{ k8s_namespace }} logs
    # job/registry-selftest-pull` shows an image-pull error). Telling (a) apart from (c) needs
    # one manual check the selftest can't do on its own — during/after a deploy, on daniel-box:
    # `sudo conntrack -L 2>/dev/null | grep :{{ k8s_registry_port }}` while a pull is in flight.
    # If it shows the pod receiving 10.42.0.1, this rule is load-bearing (a). If it shows
    # something else and the pull still succeeds, this rule is dead (c) — remove it.
    - from:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: {{ registry_k8s_netpol_cni_gateway }}
        # The AGENT pull path (2026-08-14, nut→k8s port): containerd on daniel-server
        # reaches the pinned ClusterIP through its registries.yaml mirror. Host-originated
        # traffic entering the overlay is SNAT'd to the node's own flannel.1 address —
        # `ip -4 addr show flannel.1` on daniel-server reads 10.42.1.0/32 (checked
        # 2026-08-14). Same (a)/(b)/(c) caveats as the loopback ipBlock above; the
        # daniel-server-pinned pull selftest is what distinguishes (b) loudly.
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: {{ registry_k8s_netpol_agent_flannel }}
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: {{ k8s_registry_port }}
